# Home Assistant app files

Packaging conventions follow [home-assistant/apps-example](https://github.com/home-assistant/apps-example). That example is a small in-container service. Privileged wrappers (`rancher/`) keep the same files but start an upstream image via Docker API.

## config.yaml

Required keys: `name`, `version`, `slug`, `description`, `arch`

```yaml
name: My App
version: "1.0.0"
slug: my-app          # must match folder name
description: "Short description"
url: "https://github.com/byte-bridge/haos-apps/tree/main/my-app"
arch:
  - aarch64
  - amd64
```

## Ingress

```yaml
ingress: true
ingress_port: 8099
ingress_stream: true   # if the app uses websockets
```

nginx must allow only `172.30.32.2` and proxy to the backend service.

## rootfs scripts

```bash
#!/usr/bin/with-contenv bashio
bashio::log.info "Starting service..."
exec /path/to/binary
```

- `cont-init.d/` — one-time setup at start
- `services.d/<name>/run` — long-running service (`exec` the main process)
- `services.d/<name>/finish` — cleanup on stop

## Dockerfile labels

```dockerfile
LABEL \
    io.hass.name="${BUILD_NAME}" \
    io.hass.description="${BUILD_DESCRIPTION}" \
    io.hass.arch="${BUILD_ARCH}" \
    io.hass.type="addon" \
    io.hass.version=${BUILD_VERSION}
```
